export default {"id":"dsp-level-5","number":5,"title":"DFT, FFT, windows, Spectrum","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"1. DTFT of a discrete-time signal is continuous in frequency and periodic with Fs (Rocchesso eqs. 10–11) 2. In practice we take N samples and compute the DFT (eqs. 18–19):","modules":[{"id":"dsp-m-6-1","code":"6","title":"overview dsp part 6","blurb":"overview dsp part 6","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-6-overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"LEVEL 5 — DFT, FFT, windows, Spectrum overview","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Objective:</strong> Read a spectrum as a DFT of a windowed block, not as a mystical analyzer."}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-5-1-2","code":"5.1","title":"— From DTFT to DFT","blurb":"1. DTFT of a discrete-time signal is continuous in frequency and periodic with Fs (Rocchesso eqs. 10–11) 2. In practice we take N samples and compute the DFT (eqs. 18–19):","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-6-1","title":"From DTFT to DFT","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Module 5.1 — From DTFT to DFT","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Write the DFT / IDFT pair and the bin-frequency formula."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Topic coverage:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["DTFT of a discrete-time signal is continuous in frequency and periodic with <code>Fs</code> (Rocchesso eqs. 10–11)","In practice we take <code>N</code> samples and compute the DFT (eqs. 18–19):   <code>Y(k) = Σ_{n=0}^{N−1} y(n) e^{−j 2π k n / N}</code>, <code>k = 0 … N−1</code> <code>f_k = k Fs / N</code>","Inverse DFT (eq. 22) recovers <code>y(n)</code>","Real signals: conjugate symmetry. Spectrum devices typically display <code>0 … Fs/2</code>","Euler’s formula as the bridge from cisoids to sine/cosine (Kim tutorial Step 3; Rocchesso Appendix A)","A cosine at bin centre appears as a single pair of lines; off-bin frequencies leak (preview of windows)"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>mustCover:</strong> DFT sum; <code>f_k = k Fs / N</code>; display to Nyquist for real signals   <strong>mustNotCover:</strong> Deriving Cooley–Tukey butterflies as homework   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Rocchesso §1.3; Kim convolution tutorial (Euler / sinusoids)   <strong>Practice:</strong> At <code>Fs = 44100</code>, <code>N = 4096</code>, compute bin width <code>Fs/N</code> and the bin index nearest 440 Hz   <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> Learner computes one bin frequency and one bin width"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-5-2-3","code":"5.2","title":"— Windowing, leakage, time–frequency tradeoff","blurb":"1. A rectangular window on N samples is a discontinuity at the edges → side lobes (leakage) (Rocchesso fig. 3, bullets on p. 9) 2. Smoother windows reduce side lobes and widen the main lobe","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-6-2","title":"Windowing, leakage, time–frequency tradeoff","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Module 5.2 — Windowing, leakage, time–frequency tradeoff","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Name main lobe, side lobes, and the uncertainty relation Rocchesso states."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Topic coverage:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["A rectangular window on <code>N</code> samples is a discontinuity at the edges → side lobes (leakage) (Rocchesso fig. 3, bullets on p. 9)","Smoother windows reduce side lobes and widen the main lobe","Uncertainty: product of window length and frequency resolution is constant, <code>Δf · N = 1</code> (Rocchesso eq. 13). Longer window → finer <code>Δf</code>, poorer time localization","This is the same tradeoff Dolson later states for the phase vocoder (Level 10)","Zero-padding interpolates the DFT <em>display</em>; it does not add new measured information (standard DFT fact — if a lesson needs it, open Smith or Rocchesso; do not invent a “more resolution” claim)"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>mustCover:</strong> Leakage; main lobe vs side lobes; <code>Δf N = 1</code>   <strong>mustNotCover:</strong> A catalog of named windows (Hann, Hamming, Blackman) unless that lesson opens a source that defines them. Live Spectrum’s exact window is <code>{type:'gap'}</code> unless the Live manual states it   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Rocchesso §1.3 and §4.1.3 (windowing, referenced forward)   <strong>Practice:</strong> Compare a long sustained Operator sine (narrow Spectrum line) with a very short burst of the same sine (wider mound)   <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> Learner explains why a short window cannot separate two close tones"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"dsp-m-5-3-4","code":"5.3","title":"— FFT cost and Live Spectrum as a laboratory","blurb":"1. Direct DFT is O(N²); FFT is O(N log N) (Rocchesso; Sundararajan) 2. FFT typically assumes N a power of two (Sundararajan). Live’s Spectrum implementation details are whatever ch. 28 states — quote the manual; do not invent bin counts","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"dsp-part-6-3","title":"FFT cost and Live Spectrum as a laboratory","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Module 5.3 — FFT cost and Live Spectrum as a laboratory","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> State why the FFT exists; use Spectrum as a qualitative DFT display."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Topic coverage:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Direct DFT is <code>O(N²)</code>; FFT is <code>O(N log N)</code> (Rocchesso; Sundararajan)","FFT typically assumes <code>N</code> a power of two (Sundararajan). Live’s Spectrum implementation details are whatever ch. 28 states — quote the manual; do not invent bin counts","Laboratory method: Operator sine (one line), Operator square-ish / saw-ish (harmonic series — connect to Sound Design physics, do not reteach timbre craft), noise (broadband)","Spectrum sits after the device under test. Dry vs wet A/B","Fact Sheet honesty: we report what the meter shows, not what a listener “must” hear"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>mustCover:</strong> <code>N log N</code> vs <code>N²</code>; Spectrum as the course analyzer; sine vs harmonic vs noise signatures   <strong>mustNotCover:</strong> Claiming Spectrum’s undocumented FFT size   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Rocchesso §1.3; Sundararajan abstract; Live ch. 28 Spectrum section (quote only what it prints); Sound Design physics (harmonic series)   <strong>Practice:</strong> Three Spectrum screenshots: sine 1 kHz; same oscillator on a non-sine wave; EQ Eight notch on that tone   <strong>Checkpoint:</strong> Learner predicts which plot is sine vs broadband noise"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Gate D — Spectrum literacy","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Self-assess: write <code>f_k = k Fs / N</code>; compute one bin width; explain leakage as a window fact; identify sine vs harmonic vs noise on Spectrum."}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
